ec: reap dead stripes in stripe_repair; pace removal scans for repair

Two device-removal dead ends found while removing an evacuated device
from an EC pool (buckets with no live sectors left behind by stripe
bookkeeping, removal failing with
remove_by_backpointer_did_not_terminate):

1. bch2_stripe_repair() early-returns on degraded stripes with zero live
   data blocks - nothing to repair - without deleting the stripe. Empty
   stripes are otherwise only reaped via the stripe LRU delete work, which
   a stripe that emptied while open never reaches. The dead stripe keeps
   pinning its buckets (and its reconcile work entry) forever; on a device
   being removed those buckets can never drain.

   Fix: with an intent lock on the stripe key already held, delete
   degraded+empty stripes in place. The stripe trigger drops the bucket
   refs and the reconcile_hipri entry as with any other stripe deletion.

2. bch2_dev_data_drop_by_backpointers() runs its scans back-to-back and
   bails after 10 scans without a new bucket-count low. But the scan
   enqueues its follow-up work (stripe repair, extent re-replication) to
   reconcile, which runs asynchronously: 10 scans complete in well under a
   second, far faster than even a healthy reconcile can rewrite the
   flagged stripes - so removal fails spuriously on perfectly recoverable
   state (reproduced in ktest: 5x4G devices, 4G of EC data, remove fails
   on the first attempt in ~2s and succeeds after reconcile settles).

   Fix: on a stalled scan, wake reconcile and sleep with backoff before
   rescanning, so the async work the scan itself enqueued has wall-clock
   time to produce a new low. The stall counter still terminates a
   genuine livelock.

--- a/fs/data/ec/create.c
--- a/fs/data/ec/create.c
+++ b/fs/data/ec/create.c
@@ -2374,8 +2374,27 @@
 	for_each_data_block(i, nr_data)
 		nr_live_data_blocks += stripe_blockcount_get(old_s, i) != 0;
 
-	if (!nr_live_data_blocks)
-		return 0;
+	if (!nr_live_data_blocks) {
+		/*
+		 * Degraded stripe with no live data: nothing to repair, but
+		 * the stripe still pins its buckets - e.g. on a device being
+		 * removed, where the dead buckets stall the data-drop scan
+		 * (remove_by_backpointer_did_not_terminate).
+		 *
+		 * Empty stripes are normally reaped via the stripe LRU, but a
+		 * stripe that emptied while open never gets there: the LRU
+		 * delete work skips open stripes, and nothing re-kicks it once
+		 * they close. We hold an intent lock on the stripe key, so
+		 * just delete it - the stripe trigger drops the bucket refs
+		 * and the reconcile work entry.
+		 */
+		if (bch2_stripe_is_open(c, s.k->p.offset))
+			return 0;
+
+		try(bch2_btree_delete_at(trans, iter, 0));
+		return bch2_trans_commit(trans, NULL, NULL,
+					 BCH_TRANS_COMMIT_no_enospc);
+	}
 
 	struct bch_devs_mask devs;
 	bch2_disk_label_ec_devs(c, old_s->disk_label, &devs, le16_to_cpu(old_s->sectors));
--- a/fs/data/migrate.c
+++ b/fs/data/migrate.c
@@ -21,6 +21,9 @@
 #include "data/migrate.h"
 #include "data/move.h"
 #include "data/reconcile/trigger.h"
+#include "data/reconcile/work.h"
+
+#include <linux/delay.h>
 
 #include "journal/journal.h"
 
@@ -288,6 +291,17 @@
 			prt_printf(err, "%s(): no progress after %u scans, %llu data buckets remain\n",
 				   __func__, nr_stalled, data_buckets);
 			return bch_err_throw(c, remove_by_backpointer_did_not_terminate);
+		} else {
+			/*
+			 * The scan enqueues its follow-up work (stripe repair,
+			 * re-replication) to reconcile, which runs asynchronously;
+			 * back-to-back scans outpace it and falsely declare a stall.
+			 * Give reconcile wall-clock time before the next scan; the
+			 * stall counter still terminates a genuine livelock.
+			 */
+			bch2_trans_unlock(trans);
+			bch2_reconcile_wakeup(c);
+			msleep(100 << min(nr_stalled, 4u));
 		}
 
 		/*
